  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday night through Saturday)\nIssued at 135 AM EDT Sun Aug 23 2026\n\nFor the middle to end of this coming week, surface and low level \nhigh pressure centered over the western Atlantic will begin to \nexpand back over the region and cause winds to shift back to an \neasterly or east-southeast direction. This easterly to east-\nsoutheast regime will produce a pattern that is common in the \nsummertime across South Florida with convection being driven by the \nsea breeze circulations and being steered towards the Gulf coast \nareas. At the same time, the weak SAL will dissipate by mid-week and \nallow for the deeper moisture to be utilized for widespread shower \nand thunderstorm development. Highest rain chances (60-80%) each day \nwill be across Southwest Florida and this will last through the end \nof the long term period. There will be a chance for an upper level \nshortwave to develop and advect across the region late in the week, \nwhich could cause convection to become widespread across the entire \nSouth Florida forecast area. However, uncertainty remains high in \nregards to this feature. \n\nHigh temperatures are projected to remain in the low to mid 90s each \nday. Overnight lows range from the low 70s across the interior and \nlake regions to the upper 70s and low 80s along the coastal metros.",
